{%- comment -%}
  The article-body processor. Takes the merchant's rich-text HTML and returns it with the blog's
  components expanded. ONE source of truth: dev-article renders the post through it and the
  component library page (dev-article-components) renders its live examples through it, so a
  component can never look one way in the library and another in a post.

  THE EDITOR'S INTERFACE IS SHORTCODES, typed as plain text in Shopify's visual editor — no HTML
  view, no classes, nothing an editor can break by pasting from Google Docs:

    [tip]  …paragraphs…  [/tip]      Top-tip box, sun icon. First paragraph inside = its title.
    [tip:leaf] … [/tip]              Same box, any icon: a built-in name or an uploaded
                                     "Article icon" (Content -> Metaobjects).
    [quote] … [/quote]               Quote box. The editor's own "Quote" format does the same.
    [icon:wash-40] Wash at 40°C      Inline icon. A bullet list whose items start with one
                                     becomes the icon breakdown (CSS :has(), no class needed).
    [caption] A fresher bed.         Caption under the image just above it.
    <h2>[plain]The bottom line</h2>  Keeps a heading out of the "1. 2. 3." numbering.

  Why shortcodes and not HTML snippets with classes: classes and data-* attributes only survive
  if the editor switches to the HTML view and pastes precisely, and a round-trip through Google
  Docs strips them. A bracketed word survives every editor, every paste and every copy-edit.

  ORDER MATTERS below: components first, heading anchors last, so the anchors are built from the
  final markup the table of contents (in dev-article) reads back.

  @param {string} html - the raw article HTML (article.content, or example markup)
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign out = html

  comment
    Lazy-load body images. The first duplicate attribute wins, so an editor-set loading="eager"
    still ends up lazy and a loading="lazy" is unchanged. See dev-article's note on the hero LCP.
  endcomment
  assign out = out | replace: '<img ', '<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" '

  comment
    Responsive, shift-free body images. The editor inserts ONE file at one size, with no width or
    height. On the store preview (2026-09-22) that meant a 1440px photo on a 390px phone, and —
    worse — each lazy photo took up NO height until it arrived, so its caption sat alone and the
    text below jumped when it loaded (a layout shift, i.e. CLS).

    Any image on the store's own CDN can be resized AND cropped by URL, so each one gets:
      * a fixed 3:2 shape, cropped on the CDN — the hero's ratio (--ratio-media), so every photo in
        a post is the same shape and the page reads as one design;
      * width/height attributes (1440x960), so the browser reserves the box before a byte arrives;
      * a srcset ladder + `sizes` stating the real box (720px measure, or the viewport less the
        16px gutters), so a phone downloads ~780w, not 1440w.
    ESCAPE HATCH: an image whose HTML already carries its own width and height keeps its real
    shape — the browser can reserve that box itself — and only gains the srcset.
    Images hosted anywhere else cannot be resized and are left exactly as written.
  endcomment
  if out contains '<img '
    assign chunks = out | split: '<img '
    assign rebuilt = ''
    assign ladder = '360,540,720,900,1080,1440' | split: ','
    for chunk in chunks
      if forloop.first
        assign rebuilt = chunk
        continue
      endif
      assign piece = chunk
      assign tag = chunk | split: '>' | first
      assign extra = ''
      unless tag contains 'srcset='
        assign src = tag | split: 'src="' | last | split: '"' | first
        assign on_cdn = false
        if tag contains 'src="'
          if src contains '/cdn/shop/' or src contains 'cdn.shopify.com'
            assign on_cdn = true
          endif
        endif
        if on_cdn
          assign base = src | split: '?' | first
          assign own_size = false
          if tag contains ' width="' and tag contains ' height="'
            assign own_size = true
          endif
          assign srcset = ''
          for w in ladder
            assign wn = w | plus: 0
            if own_size
              assign entry = base | append: '?width=' | append: wn
            else
              assign hn = wn | times: 2 | divided_by: 3
              assign entry = base | append: '?width=' | append: wn | append: '&amp;height=' | append: hn | append: '&amp;crop=center'
            endif
            assign entry = entry | append: ' ' | append: wn | append: 'w'
            if srcset == blank
              assign srcset = entry
            else
              assign srcset = srcset | append: ', ' | append: entry
            endif
          endfor
          assign extra = 'srcset="' | append: srcset | append: '" sizes="(min-width: 769px) 720px, calc(100vw - 32px)" '
          unless own_size
            assign new_src = base | append: '?width=1440&amp;height=960&amp;crop=center'
            assign quoted_old = 'src="' | append: src | append: '"'
            assign quoted_new = 'src="' | append: new_src | append: '"'
            assign piece = piece | replace_first: quoted_old, quoted_new
            assign extra = extra | append: 'width="1440" height="960" data-crop="3:2" '
          endunless
        endif
      endunless
      assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: '<img ' | append: extra | append: piece
    endfor
    assign out = rebuilt
  endif

  comment
    ---- [tip] / [/tip] ----
    Only expanded when opens and closes BALANCE. An unclosed [tip] would otherwise open a <div>
    that swallows the rest of the article into the box. Unbalanced, the shortcodes are left as
    typed, so the editor sees the stray bracket in the preview instead of a broken page.
    Counting by length difference: every occurrence of '[tip' is 4 characters removed.
  endcomment
  assign len = out | size
  assign tip_opens = out | remove: '[tip' | size
  assign tip_opens = len | minus: tip_opens | divided_by: 4
  assign tip_closes = out | remove: '[/tip]' | size
  assign tip_closes = len | minus: tip_closes | divided_by: 6

  if tip_opens > 0 and tip_opens == tip_closes
    comment
      [tip:name] — split on the shortcode itself so ANY name is read, built-in or uploaded
      (snippets/dev-article-icon.liquid resolves both). The editor's paragraph around the code,
      `<p>[tip:leaf]</p>`, is unwrapped: the trailing `<p>` is cut off the text before it and the
      `</p>` off the text after it, so the box never sits inside a paragraph.
      An unknown name still opens the box, just without an icon: dropping the OPEN while its
      [/tip] is still converted would leave a stray `</div></div>` closing the page's own markup.
    endcomment
    if out contains '[tip:'
      assign chunks = out | split: '[tip:'
      assign rebuilt = ''
      for chunk in chunks
        if forloop.first
          assign rebuilt = chunk
          continue
        endif
        assign tip_name = chunk | split: ']' | first
        assign piece = chunk | remove_first: tip_name | remove_first: ']'
        assign tail = rebuilt | slice: -3, 3
        if tail == '<p>'
          assign keep = rebuilt | size | minus: 3
          assign rebuilt = rebuilt | slice: 0, keep
          assign piece = piece | remove_first: '</p>'
        endif
        capture svg
          render 'dev-article-icon', name: tip_name
        endcapture
        assign open_tag = '<div class="larke-tip"><span class="larke-tip__icon">' | append: svg | append: '</span><div class="larke-tip__body">'
        assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: open_tag | append: piece
      endfor
      assign out = rebuilt
    endif

    if out contains '[tip]'
      capture svg
        render 'dev-article-icon', name: 'sun'
      endcapture
      assign open_tag = '<div class="larke-tip"><span class="larke-tip__icon">' | append: svg | append: '</span><div class="larke-tip__body">'
      assign out = out | replace: '<p>[tip]</p>', open_tag | replace: '[tip]', open_tag
    endif

    assign out = out | replace: '<p>[/tip]</p>', '</div></div>' | replace: '[/tip]', '</div></div>'
  endif

  comment ---- [quote] / [/quote] — same balance guard ----
  endcomment
  assign len = out | size
  assign q_opens = out | remove: '[quote]' | size
  assign q_opens = len | minus: q_opens | divided_by: 7
  assign q_closes = out | remove: '[/quote]' | size
  assign q_closes = len | minus: q_closes | divided_by: 8
  if q_opens > 0 and q_opens == q_closes
    assign out = out | replace: '<p>[quote]</p>', '<blockquote>' | replace: '[quote]', '<blockquote>'
    assign out = out | replace: '<p>[/quote]</p>', '</blockquote>' | replace: '[/quote]', '</blockquote>'
  endif

  comment
    ---- [icon:name] ----
    Split on the shortcode so any name is read: built-in first, then the store's "Article icon"
    uploads (both resolved by snippets/dev-article-icon.liquid). A name that resolves to nothing
    is put back exactly as typed, so a typo shows in the preview instead of vanishing.
  endcomment
  if out contains '[icon:'
    assign chunks = out | split: '[icon:'
    assign rebuilt = ''
    for chunk in chunks
      if forloop.first
        assign rebuilt = chunk
        continue
      endif
      assign icon_name = chunk | split: ']' | first
      assign piece = chunk | remove_first: icon_name | remove_first: ']'
      capture svg
        render 'dev-article-icon', name: icon_name
      endcapture
      if svg != blank
        # An icon that opens a bullet point: wrap the rest of that point in .larke-icon-text, so a
        # bold first line + line break renders as title over description (owner, 2026-10-05) —
        # the <li> is a flex row, and without the wrapper the title and text would sit side by side.
        assign li_tail = rebuilt | strip | slice: -4, 4
        if li_tail == '<li>' and piece contains '</li>'
          assign li_text = piece | split: '</li>' | first
          assign li_rest = piece | remove_first: li_text
          assign piece = '<span class="larke-icon-text">' | append: li_text | append: '</span>' | append: li_rest
        endif
        assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: '<span class="larke-icon">' | append: svg | append: '</span>' | append: piece
      else
        assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: '[icon:' | append: chunk
      endif
    endfor
    assign out = rebuilt
  endif

  comment ---- [caption] ----
  endcomment
  if out contains '[caption]'
    assign out = out | replace: '<p>[caption] ', '<p class="larke-caption">' | replace: '<p>[caption]', '<p class="larke-caption">'
  endif

  comment
    ---- <h2> anchors ----
    Every <h2> gets an id the table of contents links to, written as the FIRST attribute: when the
    editor's HTML already carries an id or class, the first duplicate attribute wins, so ours
    holds and the TOC (which reads `<h2 id="` back out of this output) can never miss one.
    Duplicate headings get a numeric suffix so two "Top tip" sections do not share an anchor.
  endcomment
  if out contains '<h2'
    assign chunks = out | split: '<h2'
    assign used = ','
    assign rebuilt = ''
    for chunk in chunks
      if forloop.first
        assign rebuilt = chunk
        continue
      endif

      comment
        `piece`, never `chunk`: assigning to a for-loop variable does not stick — the loop's own
        binding shadows the assign — so a stripped [plain] would reappear in the output.
      endcomment
      assign piece = chunk
      assign head = piece | split: '</h2>' | first
      assign attrs = head | split: '>' | first
      assign inner = head | remove_first: attrs | remove_first: '>'
      assign text = inner | strip_html | strip

      assign plain_attr = ''
      if text contains '[plain]'
        assign plain_attr = ' class="larke-plain"'
        assign text = text | remove: '[plain]' | strip
        assign piece = piece | remove_first: '[plain]'
      endif

      assign slug = text | handleize
      if slug == blank
        assign slug = 'section-' | append: forloop.index0
      endif
      assign probe = ',' | append: slug | append: ','
      if used contains probe
        assign slug = slug | append: '-' | append: forloop.index0
      endif
      assign used = used | append: slug | append: ','

      assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: '<h2 id="' | append: slug | append: '"' | append: plain_attr | append: piece
    endfor
    assign out = rebuilt
  endif

  echo out
-%}
